All four criteria verified by command output. Evidence below.

=== CRITERION 1 — docs-only discipline ===

(a) config.go change is comment-only (no code token changed).
Method: compiled a go/scanner-based token dumper (scanner mode 0 = comments
skipped) and diffed the token stream of HEAD's file against the working tree's.
  $ ./strip src/config_head.txt > cfg_head.tok
  $ ./strip /home/user/simplebudget/internal/config/config.go > cfg_new.tok
  $ diff cfg_head.tok cfg_new.tok
  -> [NO TOKEN DIFF: config.go is comment-only]   (867 tokens compared)
This is stronger than reading the diff: every lexical token (idents, literals,
operators, punctuation) is identical; only comment text changed.

(b) storage.go carries only attempt 1's comment block.
  $ diff st_head.tok st_new.tok
  -> [NO TOKEN DIFF: storage.go is comment-only]  (3204 tokens compared)
  $ git diff HEAD -- internal/services/storage/storage.go
  -> single added block: 12 comment lines appended to atomicWrite's doc comment
     ("The rename below replaces whatever currently sits at path, symlink or
     not: ... symlinks in the data directory are not honoured."). No other hunk.

(c) README carries only the contract prose.
  $ git diff HEAD -- README.md
  -> +2 lines at README.md:84 (one prose paragraph + blank line). No other hunk.

(d) gofmt / build / vet.
  $ gofmt -l internal/config/config.go internal/services/storage/storage.go
  -> (empty list), exit 0
  $ go build ./...   -> build-exit=0, no output
  $ go vet ./...     -> vet-exit=0, no output
  (Note: my first gofmt invocation mistakenly included README.md and reported
  "illegal character U+0023 '#'". That was my harness error — README is not a
  Go file. Re-run on the .go files only is clean, as recorded above.)

(e) Full test suite:
  $ go test ./...  -> 48 packages "ok", FAIL count = 0.

=== CRITERION 2 — README claim is TRUE as now scoped ===

Behavioral proof driving the REAL Storage.WriteFile (not a re-implementation)
in a `cp -a` copy of the repo under /tmp, with a planted test
(symlink_contract_verify_test.go): a symlink in the data dir pointing at a file
in another directory, then a save over it.

  $ go test ./internal/services/storage/ -run TestVerifyReadmeSymlinkClaim -v
  BEFORE: data/budget.json mode=Lrwxrwxrwx isSymlink=true
  AFTER : data/budget.json mode=-rw-r--r-- isSymlink=false isRegular=true
  AFTER : content at data/budget.json = "NEW SAVED CONTENT"
  AFTER : content at former target    = "ORIGINAL TARGET CONTENT"
  --- PASS

This confirms both halves of the README sentence: (i) "the link itself is
replaced with a regular file" — Lrwxrwxrwx -> -rw-r--r--, IsRegular()==true;
(ii) "the file the link pointed to stops being updated" — the former target
still holds its original bytes while the new bytes landed at the link's path.

Harness note (recorded for honesty): my first run of this test called
s.WriteFile("budget.json", ...) and the write did not land at the link. That
was MY bug, not a product bug: writeFileLocked passes the caller's `path`
straight to atomicWrite with no baseDir join, so a relative path resolves
against the process CWD. Verified against real call sites, which all pass full
paths (e.g. internal/services/dataloader/transfer_decisions.go:70
`tx.WriteFile(dl.transferDecisionsPath(), ...)`,
internal/services/retirement/settings.go:793 `sm.store.WriteFile(sm.filepath(),
...)`). Re-run with the absolute link path is the PASS shown above.

=== CRITERION 3 — SaveUserSettings comment is TRUE ===

(a) Empirical: bare os.WriteFile follows the symlink. Drove the REAL
Config.SaveUserSettings (not a stand-in) in the /tmp repo copy:
  $ go test ./internal/config/ -run TestVerifySaveUserSettingsFollowsSymlink -v
  AFTER: settings path isSymlink=true (mode=Lrwxrwxrwx)
  AFTER: content written THROUGH link into target = { "newKey": "newValue" }
  --- PASS
So the link is left in place and the TARGET is updated — the exact opposite of
atomicWrite's replace semantics, as the comment states.

(b) No call sites in the running server.
  $ grep -rn "SaveUserSettings" --include=*.go .
  -> only internal/config/config_test.go (lines 314,321,341,346,352,355,372)
     and the definition/doc-comment at internal/config/config.go:168,177.
  $ grep -rn "LoadUserSettings" --include=*.go .
  -> only internal/config/config_test.go and the definition at config.go:150,151.
  Non-Go mentions are docs only: docs/api/internal/config.md, CHANGELOG.md,
  .swarm/tier3/T6/report.md. No handler, no cmd/, no route. Claim confirmed.

=== CRITERION 4 — attempt-1 counterexample now covered ===

(a) The unconditional per-directory promise is gone. README.md:84 now reads:
"The files you keep in `data/` need to be real files, not symlinks.
SimpleBudget saves by writing a fresh file and renaming it into place, so when
it saves over a symlink, the link itself is replaced with a regular file and
the file the link pointed to stops being updated."
The first sentence is a requirement addressed to the user about files THEY
keep; the consequence clause is conditioned on the save mechanism just
described ("saves by writing a fresh file and renaming it into place, so...").
It no longer asserts a behaviour for every file in data/. Confirmed sole
symlink mention: `grep -n -i "symlink\|symbolic link" README.md` -> line 84 only.

(b) The out-of-contract path is documented at its source: internal/config/
config.go:168-176, immediately above SaveUserSettings, naming bare os.WriteFile,
the symlink-following behaviour, the absence of call sites, and the requirement
to route through Storage's staging write before wiring into a handler.

FINDING (reported, not a criterion failure — for lead/adversarial lane):
grep for bare writes in production code
  $ grep -rn "os.WriteFile(" --include=*.go internal/ cmd/ | grep -v _test.go
surfaces three further bare-write paths that land inside the data directory and
are NOT documented:
  - internal/handlers/backup/handlers.go:643,677 — writes
    <DataDirectory>/cache/plotly.min.js. Unlike SaveUserSettings this one IS
    live: cmd/server/main.go:251 registers `r.Get("/static/vendor/
    plotly.min.js", backup.HandlePlotly)`.
  - internal/services/storage/migration.go:49 (verifyFile) and :102
    (markerFile), both under s.baseDir.
(The remaining hits — storage/auth.go:93, backup/meta.go:63,
backup/service.go:121 — write a .tmp then os.Rename, i.e. they DO follow the
stage-and-rename contract, so they are consistent with the README.)
I do not treat these as falsifying criteria 2 or 4, for two reasons that are
matters of fact rather than taste: (1) all three are app-generated artifacts
(a redownloadable CDN asset cache, an encryption marker, an encryption verify
file), not "files you keep in data/", which is the README sentence's explicit
subject — this is a different kind of object from attempt 1's counterexample,
which was a user settings file; (2) the direction of the discrepancy is safe:
the README tells users not to use symlinks at all, and these paths FOLLOW a
symlink rather than replacing it, so a user who ignored the advice would not
suffer the data loss the README warns about. The lead may still wish to have
these documented; that is an adjudication call, not a criterion failure.

